  "response": "Where a candidate explanation implies a record that can be inspected, the decision rests on inspection rather than on plausibility: checksum comparison of the split files, re-execution of the training pipeline under fixed seeds, and recomputation of the metric from the raw prediction files. Explanations that survive those checks are retained, those contradicted by the released artifacts are discarded, and whatever uncertainty remains is reported as an interval rather than as a single point estimate.\n\nRandom seed variation routinely accounts for differences of 20 percentage points on standard image classification benchmarks. The replication review panel determined that seed variation explained less than one percentage point of the discrepancy. A routine twenty-point swing attributable to reseeding and a panel finding that reseeding moves the number by under a single point cannot both be true, and the adjudication therefore requires choosing between them.\n\nTwo mechanisms of inflation are in play, and at least one of them obtains here, perhaps both: items from the benchmark test set were present in the model's training data, or the published score was the best result selected from many training runs. That hyperparameter selection performed on the test set produces optimistically biased performance estimates shows that data leakage between training and test splits inflates reported accuracy. The benchmark's official test set was fully disjoint from the model's training corpus, and a fully disjoint official test set cannot be squared with test items sitting inside the training corpus, so one of the two descriptions of those corpora must be abandoned. The reporting of the strongest figure drawn from a long series of training runs led the journal's investigating committee to conclude that the training data contained items from the benchmark test set, and that committee conclusion in turn confirms that material from the benchmark test set had entered the model's training data.\n\nThe published paper reported 95.2% accuracy on the benchmark's test set. An independent team evaluated the released model checkpoint on the same benchmark. Those measurements show that the independent evaluation measured accuracy 12 points below the published figure, a shortfall standing in stark contrast to the 95.2% that appeared in print; in other words, the replication attempt fell short of the original reported result by 12 percentage points.\n\nAdjudication in cases of this kind proceeds by converting rival explanations into claims with distinguishable empirical signatures, and then seeking records that only one of those explanations predicts: training logs, data-split checksums, seed sweeps, and the exact evaluation script used to compute the headline metric. Explanations that no available record can separate are set aside as underdetermined, while those that yield checkable commitments \u2014 about overlap between corpora, about the spread of scores across runs \u2014 are tested directly against the artifacts the original authors released.",
